-- title:
--   The wealth process of a consumption-investment strategy
-- statement:
--   **The wealth process of a consumption-investment strategy** (Bäuerle & Rieder, *Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance* (2011), §9.1, p.268, PDF 278): $X_{n+1} = X_n - c_n + \varphi _n \cdot  R_{n+1}$, $X_0 = x$, for a strategy $(c_n, \varphi _n)$ adapted to the filtration of the relative risks.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $F_n = F^S_n$ is generated by $R_1, \dots , R_n$, so an adapted strategy is a pair of functions of the path $r = (R_1, \dots , R_n)$ ($Fin n \to  \mathbb R ^d$, 0-indexed: $r j = R_{j+1}$). `wealth c φ x n r` is $X_n$ along the path $r$.
-- source:
--   Bäuerle & Rieder, Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance (2011), §9.1, p.268, PDF 278

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_LQ_Supplement_CIRandom

namespace BauerleRiederMDP.LQ.Supplement

/-- **The wealth process of a consumption-investment strategy** (Bäuerle & Rieder, *Markov Decision
Processes with Applications to Finance* (2011), §9.1, p.268, PDF 278):
`X_{n+1} = X_n − c_n + φ_n · R_{n+1}`, `X_0 = x`, for a strategy `(c_n, φ_n)` adapted to the
filtration of the relative risks.

**Formalization Note.** `F_n = F^S_n` is generated by `R_1, …, R_n`, so an adapted strategy is a
pair of functions of the path `r = (R_1, …, R_n)` (`Fin n → ℝ^d`, 0-indexed: `r j = R_{j+1}`).
`wealth c φ x n r` is `X_n` along the path `r`. -/
noncomputable def CIRandom.wealth {d : ℕ} (c : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → Fin d → ℝ) → ℝ)
    (φ : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → Fin d → ℝ) → Fin d → ℝ) (x : ℝ) :
    (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → Fin d → ℝ) → ℝ
  | 0, _ => x
  | n + 1, r => CIRandom.wealth c φ x n (Fin.init r) - c n (Fin.init r) +
      ∑ k, φ n (Fin.init r) k * r (Fin.last n) k

end BauerleRiederMDP.LQ.Supplement


